<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    RDR-192 Step 7 (bead nexus-wbfpw.15): reapable(c), the ONE engine
    predicate for "this chunk is garbage collection's to take", as an
    inlinable SQL function, and the orphaning stamp that gives its grace window
    the meaning RDR-192 needs. Its first consumers are Step 8's
    gc_quarantine_orphans pair (vectors-022) and the read-only listing route;
    the periodic reaper (nexus-2x9xa) is the third.

    THE PREDICATE. A chunk row c is reapable when ALL of:

      1. c.last_written_at < now() - grace (default 30 days). last_written_at,
         not created_at (Sam, 2026-09-29, nexus-wbfpw.43): created_at is
         write-once, so a re-write would otherwise get no fresh grace.
         last_written_at is refreshed by every client write path that re-writes
         an existing chunk (vectors-020's header) AND by the orphaning stamp
         below, so the grace means "time since the chunk was last written or
         last lost an owner", i.e. how long it has been ownerless. The
         existence-partition metadata refresh (batchUpdateMetadata) is one of
         those client write paths, so it bumps last_written_at.
      2. no catalog_document_chunks row names (tenant, collection, chash) in
         ANY owner state. A tombstoned owner still has a manifest row, so a
         chunk whose only owners are tombstoned is NOT reapable: it is
         purge_trash's, which ages the tombstone, not the chunk.
      3. the chunk's collection is not a quarantine sibling
         (catalog_collections.lifecycle_state = 'quarantine', by state and not
         by name: RDR-204 retired parsing names). A quarantine sibling never has
         manifest rows and its rows take a fresh last_written_at on the move, so
         after 30 days every quarantined chunk would otherwise be reapable and a
         caller that visits quarantine-* collections would bypass
         gc_expire_quarantine's own clock and floors.

    THERE IS NO IN-FLIGHT-INDEX PIN. An earlier draft excluded chunks whose
    metadata catalog_doc_id named a document in index_state 'indexing'. No
    current writer stamps that key (RDR-108 removed doc_id from chunk metadata;
    make_chunk_metadata raises on it), so the pin was empty for every chunk the
    multi-batch writers produce, and its tests passed only on hand-stamped
    fixtures (review T2 nexus/review-wbfpw15-17-code C1 and -critique Issue 1).
    The orphaning stamp does the job without any metadata key.

    THE ORPHANING STAMP (vectors-021-2). A multi-batch re-index replaces the
    document's manifest at batch 1, so every old tail chunk is ownerless until
    a later batch re-adds it (RDR-223 Phase 2 gate critique, S2); a deleted
    file's chunks lose their owner when the client purges its manifest. In both
    cases last_written_at is OLD (the chunk was written long ago), so a chunk
    age grace would let the reaper take it at once. Two statement-level
    triggers on nexus.catalog_document_chunks (DELETE, and UPDATE of a row's
    chash or collection) set last_written_at = now() on every chunk that has just
    lost an own-collection manifest row and has none left at the end of the
    statement, in the same transaction as the manifest change. Doing it in the
    database means EVERY path that drops an owner row is covered by construction
    (writeManifestRows' replace, append's upsert of a position, purgeManifest,
    delete-collection, the FK cascade of a hard document delete, the SQL
    maintenance functions, the .nxexp import, anything added later) instead of a
    list of Java call sites that rots. The grace therefore protects a run's old
    tail for 30 days after batch 1, and a deleted file's chunks for 30 days after
    the purge. A statement that drops and re-adds the same chash stamps it too
    (the delete runs before the insert); that over-stamps, the safe direction,
    and costs nothing the re-put's own chunk upsert has not already paid.
    The two trigger functions are SECURITY DEFINER, with a pinned search_path and
    fully qualified names, unlike every function in this file's family: they stamp a
    chunk because a manifest row went away, so the stamp must not depend on the
    caller also holding column privileges on catalog_document_chunks (a role that can
    delete a manifest row has no reason to need SELECT on its chash to have the chunk
    stamped, and CatalogManifestSweepRepositoryTest revokes exactly that to prove a
    failed before-read fails open). They write only the chunks whose own manifest
    rows the statement removed, matched on the same tenant_id, so they widen nothing.
    This is the one exception to vectors-020's "never refreshed by maintenance"
    rule, and a deliberate one: that rule exists so dead chunks are not kept
    alive, and a stamp at orphaning only starts a dead chunk's clock.

    PARAMETERS. p_grace is an interval; NULL means the default (30 days), which
    lives here and nowhere else. Tests inject small values; a delete path must
    clamp a user-supplied grace to a floor before it reaches this function (the
    SQL stays pure). ReapableConsumersScanTest enforces it: every statement that
    deletes or copies chunks under this predicate sits in a function that takes
    the exclusive sweep gate and passes NULL, with the listing route as the named
    exemption.

    SHAPE. Same as live(c) (vectors-018): a set-returning function, because a
    scalar RETURNS boolean whose body holds an EXISTS subquery can never be
    inlined, and an opaque per-row call is what the .9 round-1 EXPLAIN caught.
    It returns one row when the chunk is reapable and none otherwise, so a
    caller writes:

        EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM nexus.chunk_is_reapable(
                    c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash, c.last_written_at, NULL))

    The row's own last_written_at is PASSED IN rather than re-read, which is
    what lets the same call be the WHERE of a DELETE: after inlining, the grace
    comparison is a qual on the DELETE's own target row, so a client write that
    refreshes last_written_at while the DELETE waits on its row lock is seen when
    READ COMMITTED rechecks the new row version, and the DELETE skips it. A
    select-then-delete-by-key would not see it. The manifest sub-query is not
    rechecked by that mechanism: a manifest row committed during the DELETE is
    the sweep gate's concern (every destructive consumer takes it exclusively),
    not this function's. The function inlines but PostgreSQL does not pull the
    EXISTS up into a semi-join, so it plans as a per-row subplan with index probes
    (idx_catalog_chunks_chash and catalog_collections' primary key), the same
    plan live(c) gets; ChunkIsReapablePlanIntegrationTest pins that under nexus_svc.

    NOT A VIEW JOIN, for the reason vectors-009 and vectors-018 give.
    LANGUAGE sql, STABLE, SECURITY INVOKER (FORCE RLS on the catalog tables stays
    the isolation boundary), no function-level SET, not STRICT; EXECUTE is the
    PUBLIC default, as for chunk_live_owners.

    NO INDEX is added for last_written_at. A btree on it would stop HOT updates
    for every client re-write of a chunk (nexus-wbfpw.43 review), and the
    candidate scan is already bounded by the (tenant_id, collection, chash)
    primary key prefix. ChunkIsReapablePlanIntegrationTest asserts the schema has
    no such index.

    OTHER PREDICATES. strandedChunkCount (purge_trash Step 1) does NOT move onto
    this function: it counts chunks that HAVE a manifest row whose owners are all
    aged tombstones, the complement of arm 2 above, so the two are disjoint by
    design and together make the dead set. purge_trash keeps its own deleted_at
    grace. RDR-192 Technical Design records the same decision.

    KNOWN CONSEQUENCE AT DEPLOY. vectors-020's ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT now() gives
    every existing chunk the migration time, so for 30 days after the engine
    carrying vectors-020 is deployed nothing that already exists is reapable.

    ROW TYPE. RETURNS TABLE adds one generated jOOQ record
    (ChunkIsReapableRecord); JooqRecordReflectionFeatureTest's count moves by +1.

    No DATA EFFECT: a function and two triggers only; no row changes at deploy.
    Placed after vectors-020 and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways block (D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="vectors-021-1" author="nexus-wbfpw.15">
        <comment>nexus.chunk_is_reapable(tenant, collection, chash, last_written_at, grace): RDR-192 reapable(c) as an inlinable set-returning SQL function. One row when the chunk has no own-collection manifest row in any owner state, last_written_at is older than the grace (default 30 days) and its collection is not a quarantine sibling; no row otherwise. No call site migrated here (Step 8).</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.chunk_is_reapable(
    p_tenant          text,
    p_collection      text,
    p_chash           bytea,
    p_last_written_at timestamptz,
    p_grace           interval DEFAULT NULL
) RETURNS TABLE(reapable boolean)
LANGUAGE sql
STABLE
SECURITY INVOKER
AS $$
    SELECT true
     WHERE p_last_written_at &lt; now() - COALESCE(p_grace, interval '30 days')
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1
               FROM nexus.catalog_document_chunks m
              WHERE m.tenant_id  = p_tenant
                AND m.collection = p_collection
                AND m.chash      = p_chash
           )
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1
               FROM nexus.catalog_collections cc
              WHERE cc.tenant_id = p_tenant
                AND cc.name      = p_collection
                AND cc.lifecycle_state = 'quarantine'
           )
$$;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS nexus.chunk_is_reapable(text, text, bytea, timestamptz, interval);</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="vectors-021-2" author="nexus-wbfpw.15">
        <comment>The orphaning stamp: statement-level AFTER DELETE and AFTER UPDATE triggers on nexus.catalog_document_chunks set nexus.chunks.last_written_at = now() on every chunk that just lost an own-collection manifest row and has none left, in the manifest change's own transaction. Covers every path that drops an owner row by construction, so reapable(c)'s grace means time since the chunk became ownerless.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete()
RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = pg_catalog, pg_temp
AS $$
BEGIN
    UPDATE nexus.chunks c
       SET last_written_at = now()
      FROM (SELECT DISTINCT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM old_rows) o
     WHERE c.tenant_id  = o.tenant_id
       AND c.collection = o.collection
       AND c.chash      = o.chash
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1 FROM nexus.catalog_document_chunks m
              WHERE m.tenant_id  = c.tenant_id
                AND m.collection = c.collection
                AND m.chash      = c.chash);
    RETURN NULL;
END;
$$;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update()
RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = pg_catalog, pg_temp
AS $$
BEGIN
    UPDATE nexus.chunks c
       SET last_written_at = now()
      FROM (SELECT DISTINCT o.tenant_id, o.collection, o.chash
              FROM old_rows o
              JOIN new_rows n
                ON n.tenant_id = o.tenant_id AND n.doc_id = o.doc_id AND n.position = o.position
             WHERE n.chash IS DISTINCT FROM o.chash
                OR n.collection IS DISTINCT FROM o.collection) o
     WHERE c.tenant_id  = o.tenant_id
       AND c.collection = o.collection
       AND c.chash      = o.chash
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1 FROM nexus.catalog_document_chunks m
              WHERE m.tenant_id  = c.tenant_id
                AND m.collection = c.collection
                AND m.chash      = c.chash);
    RETURN NULL;
END;
$$;

CREATE TRIGGER trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_delete
    AFTER DELETE ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks
    REFERENCING OLD TABLE AS old_rows
    FOR EACH STATEMENT
    EXECUTE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete();

CREATE TRIGGER trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_update
    AFTER UPDATE ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks
    REFERENCING OLD TABLE AS old_rows NEW TABLE AS new_rows
    FOR EACH STATEMENT
    EXECUTE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update();
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_update ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks;
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_delete ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update();
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete();
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
